  "problemStatement": {
    "formal": "For k-way birthday collisions over d days, the model records threshold powers of the form k! * d^(k-1), verifies the k=2,3,4 special cases, and proves that the scaling exponent (k-1)/k tends to 1.",
    "plain": "Backfills metadata for the asymptotic scaling entry in the k-way birthday problem. The proof computes concrete threshold powers, compares them with pigeonhole-style bounds in small cases, and proves that the exponent approaches the linear pigeonhole exponent as k grows.",
    "whyMatters": [
      "The ordinary birthday paradox has a square-root threshold; k-way collisions interpolate toward near-linear thresholds.",
      "Explicit k=2, k=3, and k=4 formulas make the asymptotic scaling easy to search and compare.",
      "The exponent-limit theorem captures the qualitative transition from pair collisions to high-multiplicity collisions."
    ]
  },
  "knownResults": {
    "proven": [
      "`threshold_k2`, `threshold_k3`, and `threshold_k4` compute the modeled threshold powers for 2-way, 3-way, and 4-way collisions.",
      "`pigeonhole_vs_threshold_k2` and `pigeonhole_vs_threshold_k3` compare the thresholds with pigeonhole-style bounds in clean cases.",
      "`concrete_365_k2` records the standard 365-day k=2 threshold-power value.",
      "`exponent_approaches_one` proves that the scaling exponent (k-1)/k approaches 1.",
      "The exact `BirthdayProblemOQ03OQ03.lean` file is recorded with 0 axioms and 0 sorries."
    ],
    "open": [
      "Sharper probability-limit constants for the full k-way collision distribution are handled by sibling files and are not restated here."
    ],
    "goal": "Expose the k-way birthday-threshold scaling proof through complete problem metadata."
